#figure(image("figures/retrieval_js.png", width: 100%), caption: [Retrieval on five held-out JavaScript repositories (mean with 95% bootstrap interval; higher is better). BM25 wins on locate; the graph ranker wins on dependency; fusion helps on impact but not beyond the graph ranker.])

#figure(image("figures/retrieval_py.png", width: 100%), caption: [The same comparison on four Python repositories. The pattern matches JavaScript.])

*Locate.* BM25 is the best ranker in all three groups. On the held-out JavaScript repositories the hybrid's MRR is 0.280 lower than BM25's (95% interval \[−0.373, −0.188\], p < 0.0001). On Python it is 0.167 lower (\[−0.232, −0.103\]). Adding BM25 into the hybrid closes part of that gap but not all of it (held-out −0.168, p = 0.0003). The hybrid does beat the Vector ranker alone on the post-hoc JavaScript set (+0.078, p = 0.009) and the Python set (+0.104, p = 0.003), but not on the held-out set (+0.020, \[−0.082, 0.120\], p = 0.71).

*Dependency.* The graph ranker is best. On the held-out repositories its MRR is 0.974 against 0.640 for BM25 (a difference of +0.333, p < 0.0001). The hybrid is much better than BM25 and Vector, but not better than the graph ranker it contains (−0.059, \[−0.134, 0.010\], p = 0.14). Note that this question type is almost a lookup in the import graph, and the answer key is also built from import relations, so the graph's score mostly reflects how accurate the analyzer is (Section 5.3), not how good it is at searching.

*Impact.* The graph ranker clearly beats the text-based rankers: held-out nDCG\@10 is 0.744 against 0.557 for BM25, and the gap is larger on the post-hoc set (0.907 against 0.416) and on Python (0.938 against 0.431). On the held-out set the hybrid improves on the graph ranker in recall (+0.141, \[0.027, 0.277\], p = 0.032) but not in nDCG (+0.106, \[−0.003, 0.234\], p = 0.092). On the post-hoc set (−0.019, p = 0.074) and the Python set (−0.011, p = 0.14) it is indistinguishable from the graph ranker.

*Summary.* Structural questions need the structural (graph) ranker. Fusion beats the keyword and vector rankers on them only because the graph ranker is part of it, and it does not beat the graph ranker alone. For "where is this implemented?" no semantic, graph or fused ranker beats BM25. The Vector ranker, which uses latent semantic analysis and not a neural model, is weaker than BM25 everywhere.

== 5.2 Graph Path Pruning (RQ2)

#tbl([Execution-flow reconstruction on the 25 held-out flows. A flow question asks which file-to-file dependency edges carry a behaviour. "Edges returned" is the average number of edges a method outputs; the ideal answer has two or three. The best F1 is bold on green.])[
  #flows-held-out
]

#figure(image("figures/flows.png", width: 100%), caption: [Execution-flow reconstruction on the 25 held-out flows. Left: F1 with 95% bootstrap interval. Right: edges returned per question. Pruning shrinks the output by about 88%, but only the post-hoc hybrid variant (hatched) is above the simple top-3 baselines, and not significantly.])

*Output size.* The primary pruner returns 87.8% fewer edges than unpruned traversal (1.9 against 15.7 per question). Its F1 is higher than the unpruned traversal (+0.119, \[−0.009, 0.259\], p = 0.097), but the difference is not significant at the 0.05 level.

*Against simple baselines.* The primary pruner scores below the "top 3 by path words" baseline (−0.100, \[−0.280, 0.079\], p = 0.30) and below the "top 3 by BM25" baseline (−0.056, p = 0.31). Neither difference is significant, but neither supports the pruner either. On the four JavaScript library questions the primary pruner returned no edges at all. The reason is that reciprocal-rank relevance values are smaller than path-word overlap values, so few edges reach the fixed threshold of 2.0. This is a flaw in how the scores are scaled. We left it unfixed so that the held-out result stays untouched.

*Layering and continuity.* Switching off the layering bonus changes nothing on the held-out set (difference 0.000). That is expected, because most benchmark repositories are libraries and not layered route, controller and service applications. Switching off the continuity bonus actually raises F1 (0.264 against 0.200, p = 0.125). So we cannot claim that either part helps.

*Fused relevance (exploratory).* Taking relevance from the fused BM25, Vector and Graph ranking gives the best F1 of all (0.353). That is +0.272 over unpruned traversal (\[0.161, 0.380\], p = 0.0001) and +0.097 over the "top 3 by BM25" baseline (\[0.023, 0.177\], p = 0.031), but only +0.053 over the "top 3 by path words" baseline (\[−0.091, 0.197\], p = 0.50). We chose this variant after seeing the held-out results of the other variants, so it needs a fresh question set before it counts as confirmed. On the 23 dev flows it only ties the path-words baseline (0.325 each).

== 5.3 Multi-language analysis (RQ3)

#tbl([How well the analyzer reproduces independent reference tools. "Found" is the share of the reference items the analyzer also reports; "correct when reported" is the share of what it reports that the reference confirms.])[
  #accuracy
]

#figure(image("figures/accuracy.png", width: 100%), caption: [Import-edge recall per repository against the independent oracles. The vertical axis starts at 0.85 to make differences visible.])

Import recall for each Python repository is 0.922 (`requests`), 0.983 (`click`), 0.963 (`flask`) and 1.000 (`httpx`). Our first Python resolver only looked in a file's parent directories for the package folder. It found just 0.637 of the import links, because it missed packages that sit in a different source tree, such as `import click` in `examples/` when the package lives at `src/click`. We added the dotted-name matching fallback after seeing this comparison, so the 0.968 figure is not a blind result. The remaining JavaScript misses are mostly `export ... from` re-exports, which the graph stores as exports and not as imports. Analysis took 0.1 s to 5.4 s per repository, and no repository failed.

*Python retrieval.* The findings match JavaScript (Table 3). BM25 is best for locate (MRR 0.851 against 0.683 for the hybrid). The graph ranker is best for dependency (0.949) and impact (nDCG\@10 0.938). The hybrid is no better than the graph ranker for impact (−0.011, p = 0.14).

= 6. Threats to validity and limitations

+ *Answer keys overlap with the graph.* The correct answers for dependency and impact questions come from import relations, which is the same information the graph holds. High graph scores therefore measure analyzer accuracy and the usefulness of the relation, not a hard search problem.
+ *Locate questions favour text search.* They are comment sentences, so they reward matching words inside a file. Open-ended conceptual questions are not covered.
+ *LSA is not a neural embedding.* Our "semantic" ranker is weak. A pretrained code-embedding model could change the Vector and Hybrid rows, and we did not test one.
+ *The flow benchmark is small and written by us.* There are 48 questions with two or three correct edges each, on seven repositories that are mostly libraries. The annotator also wrote the pruner, so another person should re-annotate the questions.
+ *Some choices were made after seeing results.* The content-based pruner was designed after studying mistakes on the dev set, the hybrid-relevance variant after the held-out run, and the Python name-matching fallback after an accuracy comparison. Each is marked where it appears.
+ *Pruner constants were not optimised.* We fixed the thresholds and weights in advance and did not tune them. That exposed a scaling problem (empty outputs).
+ *What this report leaves out.* We did not build a language-model-only baseline or a "function relationships" category (callers and callees). We did not evaluate answers written by a language model, and we ran no user study.
+ *Statistics.* We ran many comparisons with no correction, on samples of 25 to 145 questions.

= 7. Conclusion

We implemented and measured the full retrieval stack. The graph is essential for dependency and impact questions, and plain keyword search is hard to beat for finding where code lives. Merging the vector and graph rankings did not improve on the better of the two for either kind of question. Graph Path Pruning cut the output by about 88%, but in the form we declared in advance it did not beat a top-3 baseline on held-out flows. A variant that scores edges using the fused ranking looks promising (F1 0.353) and needs confirming on new questions. Python support matches the standard library on definitions and recovers 96.8% of imports. Future work: try a neural embedding model, have someone else annotate the flows, fix the pruner's threshold scaling, and test with a real language model and with users.

